<title>AUSTRALIA: RTRS-Australian Stocks to Watch Today - March 21.</title>
<headline>RTRS-Australian Stocks to Watch Today - March 21.</headline>
<dateline>SYDNEY 1996-03-21</dateline>
<text>
<p>Some Australian stocks to watch this morning:</p>
<p>*Broken Hill Pty Co Ltd A$17.13</p>
<p>Steel, oil and copper giant expected to report a stronger third quarter net profit. Results expected to be released about midday.</p>
<p>*Mayne Nickless Ltd A$7.98 (+.21)</p>
<p>*Seven Network Ltd A$4.40 (+.05)</p>
<p>Shares in both companies seen attracting attention following Thursday rise on rumours that a settlement to the legal action between Optus Vision and Seven, which could pave the way for Mayne's sale of its stake in the Optus group.</p>
<p>*Rothmans Holdings Ltd A$8.96 (+.04)</p>
<p>*W.D. &amp; H.O. Willis Holdings Ltd A$2.30 (steady)</p>
<p>Tobacco stocks seen lower following the settlement by U.S. tobacco group Liggett Group Inc, which admitted for the first time that nicotine is additive.</p>
<p>*Qantas Airways Ltd A$2.40 (-.05)</p>
<p>Qantas is reported to be interested in buying Ansett NZ from News Corp Ltd.</p>
<p>*Australia &amp; New Zealand Banking Ltd A$7.88 (+.16)</p>
<p>Newspaper reports that banking analysts have upgraded their forecasts for ANZ following a series of presentations, where the bank has reiterated plans for further cost cutting.</p>
<p>* Oil Search Ltd A$2.70 (-0.02)</p>
<p>* Niugini Mining Ltd A$2.92 (steady)</p>
<p>* Lihir Gold Ltd A$2.10 (+0.05)</p>
<p>* Orogen Minerals Ltd A$3.24 (steady)</p>
<p>Papua New Guinea-based gold and mining stocks are expected to remain volatile due to civil unrest over the controversial plan by Prime Minister Sir Julius Chan to use mercenaries on the island of Bougainville.</p>
<p>-- Sydney Newsroom 61-2 9373-1800</p>
